}Audit evidence notes, 2026-11-18. All parquet row numbers are zero-based. Source HEAD remained `0d362dfb61225ed457f617f76e9e59a8961b92c2`; all scoped source hashes remained unchanged. No training process was interrupted. No genuine sealed outcomes were read.

The complete manual judgments are in [visual_review.tsv](visual_review.tsv), with prompts, histories, original ScreenQA answers, strata, confidence intervals and overrides in [visual_review.json](visual_review.json). [samples.json](samples.json) retains the exact assistant reasoning and messages; [samples/](samples/) contains all 119 individually extracted PNGs and the contact sheets used for review. Each primary image was viewed alongside its goal and rolling history. Full-size follow-ups were used for qa06, qa19, qa49 and qa52. Supplemental samples cover prices, date/time and phrases absent from the simple random primary ScreenQA/dev draws; they are excluded from primary confidence intervals.

QA strata were prospective lexical classifications, six samples in each original-kind × answer-type cell. Some lexical classifications were semantically imperfect: qa14 is a duration, qa17 a filename, and dev_time_1 a listener count. Both kinds and all five actual answer types were nevertheless inspected. QA sampling deliberately oversamples rare question/phrase/name strata. The pooled Wilson interval describes the balanced audited sample, not the corpus. Population estimates weight each stratum by its endpoint count / 2,835. Approximate stratified Wilson-MOVER intervals combine each stratum's Wilson uncertainty; these are explicitly approximate, without finite-population correction. Single-reviewer interpretation uncertainty is not included in binomial intervals.

Literal answer errors are separated from goal leakage, question ambiguity and navigation mismatch. For example, dev16's 23:00 is visible, but the app identity is invented from an advertisement. ScreenQA09 loses the provider suffix `- CA`; ScreenQA27 inherits an unsupported `Home` answer from its original. Thus the shortened-ScreenQA 2/30 rate contains one incomplete entity and one inherited unsupported answer, not two errors introduced by shortening. Two further shortened answers discard another selected tab (screenqa17/18). Nine extra images had no literal-answer errors; dev_phrase_1 leaks the requested task title from its goal.

The builder was not executed. Only its pure `collect_apps`, `aw_flag`, and `new_goal` helpers and relevant constants were extracted with AST and evaluated against read-only local source metadata. [builder_closure.json](builder_closure.json) shows exact accepted-key membership, all 17,012 QA rows' eventual goals, all final screenshot hashes and direct verifier outputs agree with the implementation. Of 3,255 proposed QA labels, 89 fail the new agreement check and 169 more fail guard v2; all 2,997 accepted train/dev endpoints are present. There are 377 direct goals and 24 original-question fallbacks across train/dev; train alone has 352 direct goals and 22 fallbacks. This establishes faithful execution of the mitigations, not semantic correctness.

The app census in [missing_named_apps.json](missing_named_apps.json) matches exact known app-pool names immediately followed by `app` in goals, using the longest name first. It then checks the actual frozen Installed apps line. It is a bounded lexical contradiction census, not package identification. It finds 23,100 train rows across 4,864 original episodes and 988 QA copies, plus 62 dev_answer, 52 dev_complete and 126 dev_odyssey episodes/rows. Aliases or invented teacher app names require individual resolution. qa52 explicitly names Nike but its installed list lacks Nike. `collect_apps` reads structured `open_app` actions, not all app mentions or screen identities; maintaining one stable list does not ensure completeness.

The AW scan excludes Installed apps distractors and email-domain-only matches. [aw_exposure_candidates.json](aw_exposure_candidates.json) identifies 226 train and 15 dev QA endpoints with explicit names or excluded app types in goal/history. Restricting to Gmail, Drive, Maps and YouTube gives 205 train and 12 dev candidates. Train pattern counts overlap: Gmail 157, Drive 23, Calendar type 17, Maps 15, YouTube 11, Clock type 4. These are exposure candidates, not 226 visually confirmed AW screenshots. The reviewed Gmail/Drive/Calendar examples establish the bypass independently. Fifty-one of 700 RL QA states match this candidate set. No copied AW task is established. [aw_template_scan_extended.json](aw_template_scan_extended.json) scans 57 static class templates, 25 information-retrieval prompts and 18 dynamic-goal literal forms against 27,277 unique built goals: no full-template or eight-word fixed-span matches. Runtime compositions and paraphrases are outside this bounded test.

The RL pool's cached pinned commit has exactly 700 QA, 500 complete, 300 other and 500 click/long-press states. All prompts, images, action metadata and deterministic membership match the builder; no ScreenQA, QA prefixes, duplicate fingerprints or dev image hashes occur. All 500 completion examples are terminal AndroidControl `complete` rows. [rl_cache_identity.json](rl_cache_identity.json) corrects one evidence-field naming mistake: `expected_content_sha256` in the earlier verification JSON is the global storage key, not the LFS digest. The snapshot's direct symlink identifies `fa4e9c2ad630cdd0077f3673ec0da7dc3a793a863f02b4c9dc7a70534f34e8ae`, which matches the actual file SHA256. Shell DNS prevented a live Hub recheck; this audit verifies the local cached commit and the user-designated exact SFT copy.

The GRPO wiring agrees at source level with [official TRL 1.16 trainer](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_trainer.py), [config](https://github.com/huggingface/trl/blob/v1.16.0/trl/trainer/grpo_config.py), and [Liquid's LFM3-VL notebook](https://github.com/Liquid4All/cookbook/blob/main/finetuning/notebooks/lfm3_vl_grpo_with_trl.ipynb), archived alongside this note. The trainer accepts `image` or `images` and inserts images into conversational prompts (lines 2400–2427), passes `chat_template_kwargs` (1745), and uses the disabled adapter as the reference for PEFT (986–990, 2727). Thus a fresh LoRA on correctly merged v4 gives a v4 KL reference. The local runner asserts that every trainable tensor is language-model LoRA. `enable_thinking=True`, beta 0.001, LR 1e-5, rank 16/alpha 32, bs 4 / accumulation 8 / generations 4 are coherent. At one GPU, config defaults give generation batch 32: eight prompt visits and 32 completions per optimizer step, at most 1,600 visits / 6,400 completions for 200 steps. This is not necessarily 1,600 distinct states. No GPU execution, backpropagation, deployed dependency version or actual merged-model identity was verified.

RL operational risks beyond the ranked blockers:

- The four-step smoke measures only three step intervals on the base model, with no periodic save (save_steps=25). Main steps use this estimate, not measured v4 time; `max(10, ...)` can exceed a small budget. The 16,200-second budget covers estimated optimization only, with no wall-clock timeout or reserved eval/upload time. Dead pipelines and failed servers can leave wait loops unbounded.
- `grpo_vlm.py:111–122` says the reload must produce a parseable action, but only prints its first 200 characters and unconditionally emits `RL DONE`. It retains the trainer/optimizer/model while loading a second CUDA model, adding avoidable peak memory; no observed OOM is claimed.
- Configuration filtering can silently discard unsupported parameters after a dependency change; this script neither asserts TRL 1.16 nor records package versions. All relevant fields exist in inspected 1.16 source. It logs reward metrics but never rejects all-equal/all-zero reward groups or excessive truncation. No useful-gradient calibration was demonstrated by CPU inspection.
- Click reward is Gaussian distance to one annotated point, not element membership or action success. Nearby wrong elements get positive reward, alternative valid actions get no credit, and static reward does not establish task completion. `input_text` lowercases/collapses whitespace: all 43 capitalization-bearing texts among 72 actual pool text inputs accept lowercase variants. Some are harmless; synthetic case-sensitive filenames/secrets show the failure mode. Numeric-equivalent `1.50`/`1.5` and `10`/`10.0` get zero reward.
- The RL launcher evaluates five dev splits and AndroidControl but does not run the seed-30 gate or enforce an offline promotion rule. PREREG_v4 requires that rule before RL finishes; no adopted numeric RL rule appears there. AC test uses an unpinned dataset revision. Avoid treating reward gains or `RL PIPELINE DONE` as promotion evidence.

Remaining prior P2s: `vm_model_pipeline.sh:131,158,176` still permits cached results without binding prompt/pool identity; `vm_v4_teacher.py` still lacks a robust resume path and incremental ScreenQA checkpointing (relevant to a rebuild, not the finished artifact). Stable within-episode app lists and honest step-sampling documentation are fixed; all 2,835 built QA final histories contain the expected contiguous last-eight indices. Dev's train-derived provenance is disclosed, but these episodes were exposed to v3 and cannot support a clean held-out v3/v4 comparison. Filtering dev_complete by the original regex kind leaves information-seeking controls: episode 13412 says “I need to know” exchange rates, and 17404 says “I want to know” exchange-rate history, yet both gold actions are complete. [dev_complete_goals.json](dev_complete_goals.json) includes exact rows. The dataset README also compresses the two dev counts into “162” although dev_complete has 135.

All wrapper reproductions are synthetic and isolated below [wrapper_fixtures/](wrapper_fixtures/). Copies differ only in absolute workspace roots. Stubs perform no networking, training, process killing or emulator work. The current sealed v2 runner returns failure and confines the synthetic outcome to its synthetic sealed log; the development wrapper and RL launcher falsely report completion. The gate's zero-accuracy rejection works, but fabricated matching task-name sets and self-supplied dev keys/GT still pass. See [wrapper_reproductions.json](wrapper_reproductions.json).

